#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:2
#, no-c-format
msgid "Toolbars"
msgstr "Barras d'espleches"

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:4
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This section describes &krita;'s toolbars. By default, the <guilabel>"
"Krita</guilabel> toolbar is located to the left of the drawing area, while the "
"others can be found at the top, below the menu bar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:8
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can customize your toolbars by choosing <menuchoice><guimenu>"
"Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure Toolbars...</guimenuitem></menuchoice> "
"or by clicking with the &RMB; on a toolbar and choosing <guimenuitem>"
"Configure Toolbars...</guimenuitem>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:14
#, no-c-format
msgid "The <guilabel>File</guilabel> Toolbar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>File</guilabel> toolbar</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:24
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>File</guilabel> toolbar</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:26
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>File</guilabel> toolbar</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:31
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This toolbar contains actions for working with files. In &krita;'s default, "
"there are five buttons on this toolbar: <guilabel>New</guilabel>, <guilabel>"
"Open</guilabel>, <guilabel>Save</guilabel>, <guilabel>Print Preview</guilabel>"
", and <guilabel>Print</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:36
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"These actions all correspond to entries in the <link "
"linkend=\"commands-menus-file\"><guimenu>File</guimenu> menu</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:42
#, no-c-format
msgid "The <guilabel>Edit</guilabel> Toolbar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:46
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Edit</guilabel> toolbar</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:52
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Edit</guilabel> toolbar</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:54
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Edit</guilabel> toolbar</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:59
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This toolbar contains editing actions. With default settings this toolbar "
"offers four buttons: <guilabel>Undo</guilabel>, <guilabel>Redo</guilabel>"
", <guilabel>Cut</guilabel>, and <guilabel>Copy</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:64
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"These actions all correspond to entries in the <link "
"linkend=\"commands-menus-edit\"><guimenu>Edit</guimenu> menu</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid "The <guilabel>Navigation</guilabel> Toolbar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:73
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Navigation</guilabel> toolbar</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Navigation</guilabel> toolbar</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:81
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Navigation</guilabel> toolbar</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This toolbar offers easy access to navigation actions. The two default actions "
"available are <guilabel>Zoom In</guilabel> and <guilabel>Zoom Out</guilabel>"
". With <guilabel>Zoom In</guilabel>, the zoom level is increased. You will see "
"less, but in higher detail. With <guilabel>Zoom Out</guilabel>"
", the zoom level is decreased, so that you see more at less detail."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid "The <guilabel>Krita</guilabel> Toolbar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:100
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Krita</guilabel> toolbar</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Krita</guilabel> toolbar</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:108
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Krita</guilabel> toolbar</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This toolbar contains painting operations and tools, as well as editing and "
"selecting tools. The available actions and some controls are listed below. You "
"can change the behaviour of most tools (and with that, usually the resulting "
"effect) by setting their <link linkend=\"commands-palettes-controlbox-tool\">"
"options</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid "Brush"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:126
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can paint freely. Click the &LMB; to paint a single instance "
"of the currently selected brush, or hold the &LMB; and drag your mouse around "
"to paint. The mouse movements you make are directly used for painting."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:135
#, no-c-format
msgid "Line"
msgstr "Linha"

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:136
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool is used to draw lines. Click the &LMB; to indicate the first "
"endpoint, keep the button pressed, drag to the second endpoint and release the "
"button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:140
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Use &Shift; while holding the mouse button to restrict drawing to only "
"horizontal or vertical lines. You can press &Alt; while still keeping the &LMB; "
"down to move the line to a different location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:148
#, no-c-format
msgid "Rectangle"
msgstr "Rectangle"

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:149
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool can be used to paint rectangles. Click and hold the &LMB; to indicate "
"one corner of the rectangle, drag to the opposite corner, and release the "
"button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:153
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you hold &Shift; while drawing, a square will be drawn instead of a "
"rectangle. Holding &Ctrl; will change the way the rectangle is constructed. "
"Normally, the first mouse click indicates one corner and the second click the "
"opposite. With &Ctrl;, the initial mouse position indicates the center of the "
"rectangle, and the final mouse position indicates a corner. You can press &Alt; "
"while still keeping the &LMB; down to move the rectangle to a different "
"location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can change between the corner/corner and center/corner drawing methods as "
"often as you want by pressing or releasing &Ctrl;, provided that you keep the "
"&LMB; pressed. With &Ctrl; pressed, mouse movements will affect all four "
"corners of the rectangle (relative to the center), without &Ctrl;, one of the "
"corners is unaffected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:171
#, no-c-format
msgid "Ellipse"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:172
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Use this tool to paint an ellipse. The currently selected brush is used for "
"drawing the ellipse outline. Click and hold the &LMB; to indicate one corner of "
"the <quote>bounding rectangle</quote> of the ellipse, then move your mouse to "
"the opposite corner. &krita; will show a preview of the ellipse using a thin "
"line. Release the button to draw the ellipse."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:178
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you hold &Shift; while drawing, a circle will be drawn instead of an "
"ellipse. Holding &Ctrl; will change the way the ellipse is constructed: instead "
"of two corners, the initial mouse position indicates the ellipse center, and "
"the final mouse position indicates one of the corners of the bounding "
"rectangle. You can press &Alt; while still keeping the &LMB; down to move the "
"ellipse to a different location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:185
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can change between the corner/corner and center/corner drawing methods as "
"often as you want by pressing or releasing &Ctrl;, provided that you keep the "
"&LMB; pressed. With &Ctrl; pressed, mouse movements will affect all four "
"corners of the bounding rectangle (relative to the center), without &Ctrl;, the "
"corner opposite to the one you are moving remains still."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:195
#, no-c-format
msgid "Polygon"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:196
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can draw polygons. Click the &LMB; to indicate the starting "
"point and successive vertices, then double-click or press &Enter; to connect "
"the last vertex to the starting point."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:204
#, no-c-format
msgid "Polyline"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:205
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Polylines are drawn like polygons, with the difference that the double-click "
"indicating the end of the polyline does not connect the last vertex to the "
"first one."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:213
#, no-c-format
msgid "Star"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:214
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool creates star-shaped objects. Press the &LMB; to indicate the center, "
"and drag the mouse to change the size and rotation of the star."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:217
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can press &Alt; while still keeping the &LMB; down to move the star to a "
"different location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:224
#, no-c-format
msgid "Bezier"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:225
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can draw Bezier curves by using this tool. Click the &LMB; to indicate the "
"starting point of the curve, then click again for consecutive control points of "
"the curve."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:232
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Drawing a Bezier curve</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:238
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Drawing a Bezier curve</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:240
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>Drawing a Bezier curve</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:244
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; will show a blue line with two handles when you add a control point. "
"You can drag these handles to change the direction of the curve in that point."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:250
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Modifying a Bezier curve</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:256
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Modifying a Bezier curve</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:258
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>Modifying a Bezier curve</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:262
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can click on a previously inserted control point to modify it. With an "
"intermediate control point (&ie; a point that is not the starting point and not "
"the ending point), you can move the direction handles separately to have the "
"curve enter and leave the point in different directions. After editing a point, "
"you can just click on the canvas to continue adding points to the curve."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:269
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Pressing <keycap>Delete</keycap> will remove the currently selected control "
"point from the curve. Double-click the &LMB; on any point of the curve or press "
"&Enter; to finish drawing, or press &Esc; to cancel the entire curve. You can "
"use &Ctrl; while keeping the &LMB; pressed to move the entire curve to a "
"different position."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:278
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>A finished Bezier curve</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:284
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>A finished Bezier curve</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:286
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>A finished Bezier curve</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:293
#, no-c-format
msgid "Duplicate"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:294
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can use this tool to duplicate parts of an image. Press &Shift; and click "
"with the &LMB; on the location you want to duplicate from. &krita; will "
"indicate this location by an outline of your current brush. Then click with the "
"&LMB; to designate the location where you want to duplicate to, and drag with "
"the mouse. You will then duplicate whatever is at the source location to the "
"current (destination) location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:301
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"While you are painting the duplicate, both your cursor in the destination "
"location and the brush outline in the source location will move, in order to "
"give you visual feedback."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:305
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can also use this tool to correct colors in a part of the image: use the "
"<guilabel>Healing</guilabel> option for that."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:312
#, no-c-format
msgid "Paint with filters"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:314
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool allows you to pick a filter and draw with it. The image below shows "
"the effect of using a large circular brush and painting with, from left to "
"right, the Maximize Channel, Minimize Channel, Invert, and Desaturate filters."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:321
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Painting with filters</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:327
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Painting with filters</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:329
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>Painting with filters</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:336
#, no-c-format
msgid "Crop"
msgstr "Rosegar"

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:337
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can crop a layer or an image to a certain rectangular area. "
"Click and drag with the &LMB; to define an area. This area is designated by an "
"outline with 8 handles. You can then use the handles to change the size of the "
"area which the image or layer is to be cropped to. You can also click and drag "
"inside the area to move the outline in its entirety."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:343
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Double-click outside the area (i.e. on a part of the image that is to be "
"removed) to confirm the cropping operation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:350
#, no-c-format
msgid "Move"
msgstr "Desplaçar"

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:351
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool, you can move the current layer or selection by dragging the "
"mouse."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:358
#, no-c-format
msgid "Transform"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:359
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can quickly transform the current selection or layer. "
"Handles will appear at the corners and sides, with which you can resize the "
"selection or layer. You can perform rotations by moving the mouse above or to "
"the left of the handles and dragging it. You can also click anywhere inside the "
"selection or layer and move it by dragging the mouse."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:369
#, no-c-format
msgid "Perspective Transform"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:370
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool allows you to change the perspective of an image. Designate the area "
"which should become the new image by clicking at its top-left, top-right, "
"bottom-right and bottom-left corners. The area given by these four corners will "
"then be transformed so that the given corners become the corners of the actual "
"image."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:380
#, no-c-format
msgid "Contiguous Fill"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:381
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Use this tool to fill a contiguous area of one color with the current "
"foreground color or a pattern. Simply click to fill up the area."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:388
#, no-c-format
msgid "Gradient"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:389
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool fills the current layer or selection with the currently selected "
"gradient. Click the &LMB;, hold it, and drag the mouse to define two endpoints. "
"The gradient will be drawn along this line. If the line does not extend to the "
"border of the selection or layer, the color at the corresponding endpoint of "
"the gradient will be used to fill up the rest of the area at that side."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:399
#, no-c-format
msgid "Text"
msgstr "Tèxt"

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:400
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can add simple text to your image. Click the &LMB; on the "
"location at which you want have the text. Then enter the desired text in the "
"dialog window that appears. The text will be horizontally centered on, and the "
"top of the text will be at the same height as, the chosen location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:409
#, no-c-format
msgid "Color Picker"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:410
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can find the color values of a point. Click the &LMB; "
"somewhere in the image to see color information about that point in the "
"<guilabel>Control box</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:418
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guilabel>Pan</guilabel>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:419
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool can be used to navigate through your image. Click and hold the &LMB; "
"and move the mouse to scroll in a certain direction."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:426
#, no-c-format
msgid "Zoom"
msgstr "Zoom"

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:427
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Use this tool to zoom in and out. Click the &LMB; to increase the zoom by a "
"factor 2 (e.g. 1:1 to 2:1), click the &RMB; to decrease the zoom by a factor 2 "
"(e.g. 1:1 to 1:2)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:435
#, no-c-format
msgid "Perspective Grid"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:436
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can create and edit a perspective grid with this tool. Click the &LMB; and "
"drag the mouse to indicate the first two corners of the grid, then click for "
"the third and fourth corners. The outline of the grid is now shown and you can "
"edit it if you are not completely happy. When you switch to a different tool, "
"the perspective grid will be subdivided and shown as thin gray lines."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:443
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you only see three corners instead of four, you probably clicked instead of "
"dragging initially. In this case you can still click the handle of your now "
"combined first and second corners and drag it to get four separate corners."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:447
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Clicking the <guilabel>Perspective Grid</guilabel> tool again later will allow "
"you to modify the grid. You can hide or remove the grid by choosing the "
"<guimenuitem>Hide Perspective Grid</guimenuitem> or <guimenuitem>"
"Clear Perspective Grid</guimenuitem> options from the <guimenu>View</guimenu> "
"menu."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:456
#, no-c-format
msgid "Paint Selection"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:457
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool can be used to select custom areas. The currently selected brush is "
"used to select areas: instead of painting on the image, the area is selected. "
"For more information on selections, see <link linkend=\"selections\">"
"the Selections chapter</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:467
#, no-c-format
msgid "Erase Selection"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:468
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tool works almost the same as the <guilabel>Paint Selection</guilabel> "
"tool, but a selection, if it exists at the mouse location, is removed instead "
"of created."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:477
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select Rectangular"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:478
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can use this tool to select rectangular areas. Operation is similar to the "
"<guilabel>Rectangle</guilabel> tool, and &Shift;, &Ctrl; and &Alt; can be used "
"like when painting rectangles."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:487
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select Elliptical"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:488
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can use this tool to select elliptical areas. Operation is similar to the "
"<guilabel>Ellipse</guilabel> tool, and &Shift;, &Ctrl; and &Alt; can be used "
"like when painting ellipses."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:497
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select Polygonal"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:499
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can use this tool to select polygonal areas. Operation is similar to the "
"<guilabel>Polygon</guilabel> tool, and &Shift;, &Ctrl; and &Alt; can be used "
"like when painting polygons."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:508
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select Outline"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:510
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can use this tool to select custom outlined areas. Click the &LMB; and drag "
"with your mouse, like when painting with the <guilabel>Brush</guilabel> "
"tool, to define the outline. When you release the mouse button, the outline "
"will be finished with a straight line between the current position and the "
"start position."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:521
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select Contiguous"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:522
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can select contiguous areas of a color. Click with the &LMB; "
"to select an area."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:530
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select Similar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:531
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can select multiple areas with the same color. Detection is "
"done the same as with the contiguous fill, but the areas do not need to be "
"adjacent."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:539
#, no-c-format
msgid "Magnetic Selection"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:540
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can easily select a visually distinct area. Click with the "
"&LMB; and move the mouse around the area that you want to select. If the area "
"has a well enough defined boundary, the selection will be drawn nicely around "
"it. You will see a number of control points appear, which connect the various "
"parts of the selection boundary."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:546
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you want more control over the area that is selected, press &Ctrl; to switch "
"to manual mode. You will now have to click for each control point. In manual "
"mode, you can also move control points by clicking on them with the &LMB; and "
"dragging with the mouse."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:551
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you want to return to automatic mode, simply press &Ctrl; again. You can "
"switch between these two modes as often as you like."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:558
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select Bezier"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:559
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this tool you can select an area by drawing a Bezier outline. See the "
"description of the Bezier tool for details."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:569
#, no-c-format
msgid "The <guilabel>Brushes and Stuff</guilabel> Toolbar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:573
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Brushes and Stuff</guilabel> toolbar</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:579
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Brushes and Stuff</guilabel> toolbar</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:581
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Brushes and Stuff</guilabel> toolbar</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:586
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This toolbar contains dropdown <quote>palettes</quote> "
"in which you can choose brush shapes, gradients, and fill patterns. It also "
"contains a dropdown box for painter's tools, and a tablet pressure setting."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:591
#, no-c-format
msgid "Brush Shapes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:595
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:601
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:603
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:608
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette, you can choose which brush to "
"paint with. This brush is used for painting operations like <guilabel>"
"Freehand</guilabel>, <guilabel>Rectangle</guilabel>, <guilabel>"
"Ellipse</guilabel>, etcetera. You can choose a predefined brush (in the "
"<guilabel>Predefined Brushes</guilabel> tab, shown above), or customize or "
"create one."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:617
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Autobrush</guilabel> tab</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:623
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<phrase>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Autobrush</guilabel> tab</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:625
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<para>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Autobrush</guilabel> tab</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:631
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Autobrush</guilabel> tab allows you to create a customized "
"rectangular or ellipsoid brush. You can set its height and width using the "
"<guilabel>Size</guilabel> spin boxes. The link icon controls whether the height "
"and width are forced to be the same or not. If a connected link picture is "
"shown, changing one value will automatically change the other one as well. A "
"disconnected link indicates that both values can be set independently. The "
"fuzziness of the brush can be set with the <guilabel>Fade</guilabel> "
"spin boxes. Again, horizontal and vertical values can be allowed to differ or "
"not, depending on the state of the link button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:643
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Custom Brush</guilabel> tab</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:649
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<phrase>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Custom Brush</guilabel> tab</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:651
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<para>The <guilabel>Brush Shapes</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Custom Brush</guilabel> tab</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:657
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Custom Brush</guilabel> tab of this palette lets you use the "
"current image as a brush. With the <guibutton>Add to Predefined "
"Brushes</guibutton> button, you can save it for later use."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:664
#, no-c-format
msgid "Gradients"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:668
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Gradients</guilabel> palette</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:674
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Gradients</guilabel> palette</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:676
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Gradients</guilabel> palette</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:681
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the <guilabel>Gradients</guilabel> palette, you can choose a gradient to "
"paint with using the <guilabel>Gradient</guilabel> tool. Clicking once on a "
"gradient in the palette will show a larger preview. Click it again to make it "
"the current gradient."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:685
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can create your own gradients with the <guibutton>"
"Custom Gradient</guibutton> button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:691
#, no-c-format
msgid "Patterns"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:695
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Patterns</guilabel> palette</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:701
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Patterns</guilabel> palette</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:703
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Patterns</guilabel> palette</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:708
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Patterns</guilabel> palette allows you to choose a pattern for "
"operations like Pattern fill. Click a pattern to see a preview at actual size, "
"then click it again to select it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:714
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Patterns</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Custom Pattern</guilabel> tab selected</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:720
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<phrase>The <guilabel>Patterns</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Custom Pattern</guilabel> tab selected</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:722
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<para>The <guilabel>Patterns</guilabel> palette with the <guilabel>"
"Custom Pattern</guilabel> tab selected</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:727
#, no-c-format
msgid "You can also create a custom pattern, as is shown above."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:732
#, no-c-format
msgid "Painter's tools"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:734
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With the <guilabel>Painter's tools</guilabel> dropdown box, you can select the "
"tool your painting operation should simulate. For example, you can paint with a "
"normal brush, an airbrush, or a filter."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:741
#, no-c-format
msgid "Pressure variation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: commands-toolbars.docbook:743
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This setting allows you to change &krita;'s behaviour when you use a tablet to "
"paint with. When you change the pressure on the tablet, you can choose between "
"changing the line width (<guilabel>size</guilabel>), the opacity, and the "
"darkness."
msgstr ""

